1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,763
The sixtieth anniversary of the morning a building
finished existing on the south bank

2
00:00:13,783 --> 00:00:19,330
of the Christina River fell on a Saturday, and
Raccoon City asked for a stadium.

3
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:25,479
The narrator wants to be precise about who asked. It
was not the Directorate. It was the

4
00:00:25,499 --> 00:00:31,182
city council — nine elected members, six of them
born in the valley, two of them former

5
00:00:31,202 --> 00:00:36,820
Absolute personnel released in the third cohort —
and they asked because a hundred and

6
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:43,179
four thousand people had signed a petition, and the
petition had been started by a schoolteacher.

7
00:00:43,579 --> 00:00:50,168
Wesker refused. He refused in March, in April, and
in June, and the narrator notes that

8
00:00:50,188 --> 00:00:56,853
this was not modesty: he had refused a stadium in
Lyon in 2035 and a procession in Basel

9
00:00:56,873 --> 00:01:03,537
in 2029 and every parade anybody had ever offered him
for sixty years, on the consistent

10
00:01:03,557 --> 00:01:10,146
and correct grounds that a crowd which is given a
ceremony becomes a crowd that expects

11
00:01:10,166 --> 00:01:10,450
one.

12
00:01:10,849 --> 00:01:16,749
In July the schoolteacher wrote to him. Her name was
Ruth Ochoa, she was fifty-one, she

13
00:01:16,769 --> 00:01:21,920
taught history at a school in the valley, and
her letter was two paragraphs.

14
00:01:22,439 --> 00:01:26,994
Director. I was born here in 2035, which means I was
made, along with my mother and my

15
00:01:27,014 --> 00:01:31,728
street and the school I now teach in. I found out
when I was nineteen, from the Registry,

16
00:01:31,748 --> 00:01:36,409
in a letter, and it took me about ten years to stop
being frightened of my own memories.

17
00:01:36,689 --> 00:01:41,100
I am asking you to stand in front of us for an hour
because there are a hundred thousand

18
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:45,531
people in this valley who have never once been
thanked for existing, and because you are

19
00:01:45,551 --> 00:01:48,200
the only person alive who knows
that we needed to be.

20
00:01:48,599 --> 00:01:50,469
He said yes in the same week.

21
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:57,175
Ninety-one thousand people. Another four hundred
thousand in the streets outside on screens.

22
00:01:57,195 --> 00:02:03,018
Nine hundred million watching live, which was not a
record — the Eleven Days had been

23
00:02:03,038 --> 00:02:09,343
four billion — but which was the largest audience any
human being had ever had for something

24
00:02:09,363 --> 00:02:11,199
that was not a catastrophe.

25
00:02:11,599 --> 00:02:17,464
There was no support programme. No music, no
warm-up, no video. The stadium lights came

26
00:02:17,484 --> 00:02:23,282
up at eight o'clock and a man in a black coat walked
out to a single microphone at the

27
00:02:23,302 --> 00:02:29,234
centre circle, alone, and the narrator would like you
to understand that he had prepared

28
00:02:29,254 --> 00:02:30,722
this for eleven weeks.

29
00:02:31,123 --> 00:02:36,897
For sixty years there had been a voice in his skull
that knew how to do this. It had told

30
00:02:36,917 --> 00:02:42,560
him in a hotel in Basel in 2029 that the pause was
everything. It had told him in a car

31
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:48,093
in 2034 to let a senator's question sit in the air.
It had been silent since ten past

32
00:02:48,113 --> 00:02:53,692
four in the morning on the fifth of December 2062,
and it was not coming back, because

33
00:02:53,712 --> 00:02:58,313
it was not a passenger any more and had not
been for twenty-four years.

34
00:02:58,713 --> 00:03:03,563
He had to do it himself, and he knew exactly
how, and that is the horror of it.

35
00:03:04,633 --> 00:03:14,355
Sixty years ago this morning, at two minutes to six, a
building finished existing in Wilmington,

36
00:03:14,375 --> 00:03:19,023
Delaware, on a car park, with
nobody watching.

37
00:03:19,743 --> 00:03:26,890
I woke up in it two minutes later. There were eleven
people downstairs who thought they

38
00:03:26,910 --> 00:03:33,974
had worked there for years. There was a laboratory
that had never run an experiment, a

39
00:03:33,994 --> 00:03:40,976
freezer full of samples nobody had collected, and a
door in the basement that did not

40
00:03:40,996 --> 00:03:42,293
go anywhere yet.

41
00:03:42,574 --> 00:03:49,790
I have never told anybody what that was. I am not
going to tell you tonight either. I

42
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:55,324
am going to tell you what it *means*,
because that part is yours.

43
00:03:55,604 --> 00:04:02,674
Every one of you knows somebody who was made. In
this valley it is one person in three.

44
00:04:02,694 --> 00:04:09,847
You have grandparents in a cemetery on the hill who
were born the same week as their own

45
00:04:09,867 --> 00:04:16,938
headstones. Some of you have found that out about
yourselves, from a letter, and I know

46
00:04:16,958 --> 00:04:24,192
precisely what that letter says because I wrote the
first draft of it in 2081 and it took

47
00:04:24,212 --> 00:04:25,577
me four attempts.

48
00:04:26,187 --> 00:04:32,179
(very slow) I want to say the thing that the
Registry cannot say, because it is a form

49
00:04:32,199 --> 00:04:33,298
and this is not.

50
00:04:33,577 --> 00:04:40,516
You are not less real than I am. You are more real
than I am. Every one of you has spent

51
00:04:40,536 --> 00:04:47,474
your life doing the ordinary work of being a person —
raising children, quarrelling with

52
00:04:47,494 --> 00:04:54,511
your councils, teaching history in a school on Mill
Street — and you have done it without

53
00:04:54,531 --> 00:05:01,310
ever once having been given a reason, which is more
than can be said for me, because I

54
00:05:01,330 --> 00:05:08,348
have had a reason for sixty years and it was handed
to me by something I have never seen.

55
00:05:08,627 --> 00:05:15,914
I did not make you as an instrument. I made you
because I could, and because it was faster,

56
00:05:15,934 --> 00:05:22,899
and I did not think about what it would be like to
be you. That was not cruelty. It was

57
00:05:22,919 --> 00:05:29,804
worse than cruelty; it was *inattention*, and I have
had sixty years to notice it, and

58
00:05:29,824 --> 00:05:33,658
it took a letter from a history
teacher in July.

59
00:05:34,608 --> 00:05:40,513
Ninety-one thousand people. The narrator has listened
to the recording six times and there

60
00:05:40,533 --> 00:05:42,817
is no sound in that stadium at all.

61
00:05:43,338 --> 00:05:46,118
So. Sixty years.

62
00:05:46,398 --> 00:05:54,122
The company that should not exist employs four
million people, supplies the medicine of

63
00:05:54,142 --> 00:06:01,867
eight billion, the power of half of them, and the
water of four hundred million. Nobody

64
00:06:01,887 --> 00:06:09,433
on this planet dies of anything ordinary any more.
The last famine ended in 2058. The

65
00:06:09,453 --> 00:06:16,288
last pandemic ended in sixty-one days in 2033
and there has not been another.

66
00:06:16,567 --> 00:06:22,881
I did all of that. I want to be entirely clear about
it, because there is a fashion now

67
00:06:22,901 --> 00:06:29,214
for saying that these things were inevitable, that
the century was going that way, that

68
00:06:29,234 --> 00:06:33,218
if it had not been me it would have
been somebody else.

69
00:06:33,497 --> 00:06:39,998
(very cold) It would not have been somebody else. I
know exactly what the alternative

70
00:06:40,018 --> 00:06:44,908
looked like. I have read four hundred and
eleven versions of it.

71
00:06:45,627 --> 00:06:52,124
And none of it was voted on. Not the medicine, not
the energy, not the length of your

72
00:06:52,144 --> 00:06:58,717
lives. Fourteen governments tried to take it back
from me nine years ago and they were

73
00:06:58,737 --> 00:07:05,387
right to try and they failed, and the reason they
failed is that I had built it so that

74
00:07:05,407 --> 00:07:06,997
only I could hold it.

75
00:07:07,278 --> 00:07:13,815
I am not going to apologise for that tonight. I am
going to tell you what I am doing about

76
00:07:13,835 --> 00:07:17,968
it, which is the only thing that has
ever meant anything.

77
00:07:18,367 --> 00:07:20,127
And he gave them the third thing.

78
00:07:20,648 --> 00:07:27,955
The release programme is at eleven per cent after
six years. By 2110 there will not be

79
00:07:27,975 --> 00:07:35,880
one human being on this planet who is incapable of
refusing an instruction from me, including

80
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:41,078
the four in this stadium standing
nearest to this microphone.

81
00:07:41,358 --> 00:07:49,178
The Hangzhou tribunal has ruled against the
Directorate nine times in six years. We have

82
00:07:49,198 --> 00:07:56,128
complied nine times. Two of those rulings cost
us more than the Coalition did.

83
00:07:56,408 --> 00:08:02,689
(very slow) And in sixteen years there will be
somewhere else to go. Not a colony — a

84
00:08:02,709 --> 00:08:09,286
*frontier*, with room in it for anyone who has
decided that a world where nothing changes

85
00:08:09,306 --> 00:08:14,698
and nobody dies is not a world they want to
spend three hundred years in.

86
00:08:15,528 --> 00:08:22,010
That is the whole of it. I am not going to ask you
for anything, because I have never

87
00:08:22,030 --> 00:08:27,978
once had to, and because that is the most
dangerous single fact about my life.

88
00:08:28,377 --> 00:08:34,682
He stopped. He did not step back from the microphone.
He stood in the middle of ninety-one

89
00:08:34,702 --> 00:08:40,655
thousand people with his hands at his sides and
waited, exactly as he had been taught

90
00:08:40,675 --> 00:08:46,627
in a hotel room in Basel fifty-seven years earlier
by a voice that no longer existed.

91
00:08:47,798 --> 00:08:49,077
And then they began.

92
00:08:49,477 --> 00:08:55,271
It was not his name. That is the detail everybody
gets wrong, in every account, because

93
00:08:55,291 --> 00:08:58,077
the footage is loud and the word is short.

94
00:08:58,477 --> 00:09:04,065
Ninety-one thousand people in a valley in
Pennsylvania stood up and chanted, over and

95
00:09:04,085 --> 00:09:09,673
over, for twenty-one minutes, the one word that the
schoolteacher's petition had used

96
00:09:09,693 --> 00:09:13,368
and that the Registry's letter had
not been able to say.

97
00:09:13,768 --> 00:09:15,318
*Real. Real. Real.*

98
00:09:16,378 --> 00:09:22,120
Albert Wesker stood in the centre circle and let it
go on, and did not raise a hand to

99
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:28,016
stop it, and did not speak over it, and at about the
fourth minute the cameras got close

100
00:09:28,036 --> 00:09:29,557
enough to see his face.

101
00:09:29,957 --> 00:09:35,639
There is no expression on it. There has been no
expression on it in sixty years. But at

102
00:09:35,659 --> 00:09:41,341
four minutes and eleven seconds he takes off the
black glasses — in front of ninety-one

103
00:09:41,361 --> 00:09:47,436
thousand people and nine hundred million more, for the
first time in public since a boardroom

104
00:09:47,456 --> 00:09:53,072
in Trenton in 2027 — and he looks up at the stands,
and his eyes are exactly what they

105
00:09:53,092 --> 00:09:55,496
have always been, and nobody screams.

106
00:09:55,897 --> 00:09:58,947
They chanted louder. That is on
the recording too.

107
00:09:59,346 --> 00:10:04,831
Ruth Ochoa went back to work on the Monday and found
four hundred and eleven letters in

108
00:10:04,851 --> 00:10:10,335
her school's post, and a camera crew outside, and a
class of seven-year-olds who wanted

109
00:10:10,355 --> 00:10:12,297
to know whether she was famous.

110
00:10:12,697 --> 00:10:18,023
She gave one interview and then refused every other
request for the rest of her life,

111
00:10:18,043 --> 00:10:18,966
which was long.

112
00:10:19,486 --> 00:10:24,158
I taught the history of this valley for twenty-six
years. I taught it from the Directorate's

113
00:10:24,178 --> 00:10:28,544
own curriculum, which is honest — I want to say
that, because people assume it is not.

114
00:10:28,564 --> 00:10:33,083
It says the city was placed. It gives the date. It
uses the word *made* about the people.

115
00:10:33,361 --> 00:10:37,561
And every year, for twenty-six years, there is a
child in that class who works out in

116
00:10:37,581 --> 00:10:41,780
the middle of the lesson that it means their
grandmother, and puts their hand up, and

117
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:43,666
asks me if their grandmother was real.

118
00:10:44,388 --> 00:10:48,363
(very slow) I have answered that question about
three hundred times. The answer I give

119
00:10:48,383 --> 00:10:52,359
is *yes*, and it is true, and it is not enough, and
every single one of those children

120
00:10:52,379 --> 00:10:54,356
knows it is not enough, and
they are seven.

121
00:10:54,635 --> 00:10:59,189
That is why I wrote to him. Not for the stadium. For
the answer. I have been giving the

122
00:10:59,209 --> 00:11:03,867
best answer I can for twenty-six years and I wanted
the man who made the question to come

123
00:11:03,887 --> 00:11:07,494
and stand in front of a hundred thousand
people and give one himself.

124
00:11:07,895 --> 00:11:11,196
Asked whether he had, she thought
about it for some time.

125
00:11:11,716 --> 00:11:16,013
(very slow) He said we were more real than he was.
It is a good line. I have used it in

126
00:11:16,033 --> 00:11:18,245
class and it works, and the
children like it.

127
00:11:18,525 --> 00:11:22,976
But I have thought about it for a month now, and I
notice that he answered the question

128
00:11:22,996 --> 00:11:27,600
about *us* and not the question about *him*, and that
nobody in ninety-one thousand people

129
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:30,016
noticed either, including me,
until I got home.

130
00:11:30,416 --> 00:11:35,934
He stayed after they had gone. The stadium staff
found him at nearly two in the morning,

131
00:11:35,954 --> 00:11:40,026
sitting in the fourth row of the north
stand, and left him alone.

132
00:11:41,176 --> 00:11:48,954
Devotion. Nine hundred million. Sentiment: not
measurable on this scale. Presence: ninety

133
00:11:48,974 --> 00:11:56,401
to one hundred and forty-one. Ninety billion
Points. Note: in four hundred and eleven

134
00:11:56,421 --> 00:12:04,200
worlds I have recorded this moment ninety-four times.
In ninety-one of them the host said

135
00:12:04,220 --> 00:12:05,865
his own name first.

136
00:12:07,004 --> 00:12:09,375
(very soft) And the other three?

137
00:12:10,215 --> 00:12:12,497
(flat) Two are still running.

138
00:12:12,897 --> 00:12:16,527
You have given me that answer
for sixty years.

139
00:12:16,927 --> 00:12:23,237
(flat) It has been true for sixty years. It
will stop being true in fourteen.

140
00:12:23,635 --> 00:12:29,719
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker sat in an empty
stadium at two o'clock in the morning

141
00:12:29,739 --> 00:12:35,486
and asked four more questions and received no answer
to any of them, and that fourteen

142
00:12:35,506 --> 00:12:39,846
years later, to the week, he found out
what that panel had meant.
